<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karl Renner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karl Renner was an Austrian Social Democratic politician who led the government of German-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> as state chancellor from 1918 to 1920. In April 1945, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, he formed a provisional government that declared Austria's independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He was elected the first president of the Second Republic in 1945 and held office until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-renner.jpg" alt="Karl Renner" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Renner_1-E-901_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Karl Renner was born on 14 December 1870, the 18th child of a poor ethnic German family of wine-growers in Unter-Tannowitz, now Dolní Dunajovice in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, then part of the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. Because of his intelligence he attended a selective <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> at nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikulov">Nikolsburg</a>, and from 1890 to 1896 he studied law at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a>. In 1895 he was one of the founders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a> of Nature organisation and designed its logo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Renner joined the Social Democratic Workers' Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1896 and sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsrat">Reichsrat</a> from 1907 until its dissolution in November 1918. With Otto Bauer and Adolf Braun he founded and edited the party's journal Der Kampf, and he published his ideas under pseudonyms to protect his post as the Reichsrat's librarian. He was counted a leader of Austro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>, and his book of 1904 on the social function of private law made him one of the founders of the sociology of law.</p><p>In 1918 he became the first State Chancellor of the Republic of German-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, a post he held until 1920. Like other Austrian socialists he favoured union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, but the Entente forbade it, and at St Germain he had to accept the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> and of the German-speaking parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. He was President of the National Council from 1931 to 1933. After the party was banned in 1934 he welcomed the Anschluss of 1938, and on 2 April 1938 he appealed to Austrians to vote yes in the plebiscite. His offer to serve the Nazi government was declined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 3 April 1945, as Soviet troops advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, Renner made contact with them. On 20 April the Soviets told him to form a provisional government, which took office seven days later, declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> independent of Nazi Germany and called for a democratic state on the lines of the First Republic. Communists held a third of the posts, and the Western Allies did not at first recognise it. The Federal Assembly elected Renner president on 20 November 1945. He died in Vienna on 31 December 1950. He is often called the Father of the Republics. The survivor Marko Feingold, head of the Salzburg Jewish community, said in 2013 that Renner had long been known in his party as an antisemite.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karl Renner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 27 April <b>Karl Renner</b> and Adolf Schärf of the Socialists, Leopold Kunschak of the Christian Socials and Johann Koplenig of the Communists declared Austria's secession from the Third Reich and set up a provisional government under Renner with the approval of the Red Army; the date is…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Karl Renner</b> of the Social Democrats led the government as state chancellor.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-german-austria">Republic of German-Austria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dolní Dunajovice, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,16.59&amp;year=1922" aria-label="Dolní Dunajovice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Karl Renner"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4252 5871h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4252 5871h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,16.59&amp;year=1922">What was here in 1922? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
